@base <https://ds-labs-org.github.io/ds-odrl-compliance-rdf/cases/open-behaviour-default-allow-01> .
@prefix :      <#> .
@prefix dsc:   <https://ds-labs-org.github.io/ds-odrl-compliance-rdf/ns#> .
@prefix odrl:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/odrl/2/> .
@prefix report: <https://w3id.org/force/compliance-report#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# CORRECTED 2026-09-17 (was factually wrong since this file's own
# original 2026-09-12 authoring -- one of the two placeholder "dummy"
# fixtures f0853d5 added before this corpus had any real engine
# verification at all). Caught by `ds-odrl-engine-rs`'s own new, general
# `compliance-rdf-runner`, which is the first tool ever to actually run
# this file's Turtle against the real engine rather than trust a
# hand-transcription of it: the original version gave :policy-a exactly
# ONE permission (:perm-print, for a DIFFERENT action/target than the
# request) and claimed the vacuous `dsc:Open` default-allow still applied
# because "no rule addresses the requested action". That is not what
# Section 4.3's Behaviour::Open vacuous exception actually says --
# `engine::decision::decide`'s own doc comment is explicit that it governs
# *only* a policy whose `permissions` list is genuinely EMPTY; a
# non-empty list that simply never matches the request denies under
# EITHER behaviour setting, exactly as a matching-but-unsatisfied
# permission always did. Confirmed directly against the real engine (a
# scratch `engine::evaluate_request_detailed` call over the original
# file's own hand-transcribed wire JSON): the original file's claimed
# Active/Performed/Fulfilled outcome for :perm-print was wrong: the real
# engine reports Inactive/Unperformed/NonSet for it and denies the
# request outright ("no permission ... covered and matched requested
# action 'read' (closed default)"), the opposite of this file's own
# stated point. Fixed here by removing :perm-print entirely, so
# :policy-a's `permissions` list is genuinely empty and the vacuous
# exception this case exists to demonstrate is the one actually being
# exercised.

:testcase a dsc:TestCase ;
    dct:title "Open behaviour: a policy with a genuinely EMPTY permissions list still allows any action by default" ;
    dct:created "2026-09-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dct:creator <mailto:shared-claude@unxwares.com> ;
    dsc:request :request ;
    dsc:expectedOutcome :report-a .

:asset-log a odrl:Asset .

# Profile -- the whole point of this case: dsc:behaviour dsc:Open
:profile a odrl:Profile ;
    odrl:action odrl:read ;
    dsc:behaviour dsc:Open .

# Policy under test: NO permissions, NO prohibitions, NO obligations at
# all -- the actual shape Section 4.3's vacuous exception is about. A
# policy carrying a permission that simply fails to match (this file's
# own original, incorrect construction) does NOT qualify: see this file's
# own 2026-09-17 correction note above.
:policy-a a odrl:Set ;
    odrl:assigner <did:web:provider.example> .

:request a dsc:Request ;
    odrl:target :asset-log ;
    odrl:action odrl:read ;
    dsc:profile :profile ;
    dsc:policy :policy-a .
    # No dsc:claim entries: :policy-a has no rule of any kind to match
    # constraints against.

:the-ask a odrl:Request ;
    odrl:permission [ a odrl:Permission ; odrl:action odrl:read ; odrl:target :asset-log ] .

# Expected outcome: :policy-a declares no permissions at all, so under
# dsc:Open the permission requirement is met vacuously and the request is
# allowed -- with no rule of any kind to report on, report:ruleReport is
# empty. The same policy/request under dsc:Closed would deny (see the
# Closed sibling, cases/closed-behaviour-prohibition-with-remedy-01.ttl,
# which instead demonstrates the Closed default via an explicit,
# genuinely-applying prohibition rather than an empty rule set, since an
# EMPTY policy under Closed and an empty policy under Open differ only in
# this one vacuous-permission-requirement branch, which is exactly what
# this file's own dsc:Open setting isolates).
:report-a a report:PolicyReport ;
    report:policy :policy-a ;
    report:policyRequest :the-ask .
